6045417] [17592186553468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553468 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553468 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553468 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553468 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c63b3ecc-2e05-511b-8089-51402be17be5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553469 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cbae0ca6-9a6a-501c-a55e-8ce32043c2fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553506 #edb/ref 1071 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=9{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-x-1$ have multiplicity $1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "269f2437-4ee3-5068-a652-f35cac84deec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553506 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =9{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-x-1 \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}(x+1)-1(x+1) \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-1)(x+1) \\\\  & =(3x+1)(3x-1)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(3x+1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(3x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x+1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-1$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the roots $x=-1,\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ all have multiplicity $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553518 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553518 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the polynomial $p(x)=(x-8)^{2}(x+5)^{4}(x-4)?$\n\nI. The root $x=8$ of $p(x)$ is a multiple root\n\nII. The root $x=-5$ of $p(x)$ is a simple root\n\nIII. The root $x=4$ of $p(x)$ is a simple root" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "472601ac-a114-5add-877d-c16f230ff432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553518 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial. A simple root has a multiplicity of $1,$ while a multiple root has a multiplicity that is greater than $1.$ \n\nFrom the factored form, we can tell that the roots of $p(x)$ are $x=8,$ $x=-5,$ and $x=4.$ \n\n- Statement I is true. The factor $(x-8)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=8$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $4$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-278334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553523 #edb/ref 1071 "The root of the polynomial $p(x)=8{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}$ with multiplicity $2$ is $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57a69fb9-4cd9-5d72-b269-2597060e6e43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553523 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $8{x}^{3}$ and $6{x}^{2}$ is $2{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =8{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}(4x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(4x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553532 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=5$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-x+30?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69d52e3a-df83-5812-9b09-31bab7d15cfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553532 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x=5$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-5)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $-1$ | $30$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $-5$ | $-30$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-5}={x}^{2}-x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-5)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-5)(x-3)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553565 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553565 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553565 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553565 #edb/ref 1071 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=16{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553565 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b274acdf-9a74-5462-a5c4-3ce49892bee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553565 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $16{x}^{3}$ and $-4{x}^{2}$ is $4{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =16{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}(4x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor ${x}^{2}$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(4x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ has multiplicity $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553589 #edb/ref 1071 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+3)({x}^{2}-4)^{3}$ have multiplicity $3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f56f30e8-2f9d-5691-bca5-63c90f4a552c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553589 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's factor $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =(x+3)({x}^{2}-4)^{3} \\\\  & =(x+3)((x+2)(x-2))^{3} \\\\  & =(x+3)(x+2)^{3}(x-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x+2)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=-2$ has multiplicity $3.$\n\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $3.$\n\nTherefore, the roots with multiplicity $3$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
